Name: systemAppearance

Type: property

Syntax: get the systemAppearance

Summary:
Reports whether the operating system is set to the dark or the light
appearance.

Introduced: 9.6

OS: mac, windows, linux, android, ios

Platforms: desktop, mobile

Example:
if the systemAppearance is "dark" then
    set the backColor of me to "black"
    set the foreColor of me to "white"
else
    set the backColor of me to "white"
    set the foreColor of me to "black"
end if

Description:
The <systemAppearance> property returns either "dark" or "light": the
setting of the operating system, whatever the application is drawn in.
The <appAppearance> and the <stackAppearance> never change it; the
<effective> <appAppearance> and the <effective> <stackAppearance> report
what the engine draws in.

On Windows it is the "app mode" setting (Settings > Personalization >
Colors, the AppsUseLightTheme value). On macOS it is the Appearance setting
(AppleInterfaceStyle). On Linux it is "light" unless the engine can tell
that the GTK theme is dark. Without a user interface (-ui) it is "light".

When the setting changes while the application runs, the engine sends
<systemAppearanceChanged>.

References: appAppearance (property), stackAppearance (property),
effective (keyword), systemAppearanceChanged (message)
